to continue developing pieces rapidly to exploit Black's structural weaknesses. The move N: g1-f3 is the most principled continuation. It develops the knight to an active square, reinforces control over the center (especially d4 and e5), prevents Black from easily utilizing the e5 square, and prepares for kingside castling to bring the King to safety. Moves like B: c1-g5 are tactical blunders due to the pawn on f6 (fxg5), and B: f1-c4, while good, might run into ...b5 tempo gains given ...a6.\n\nReason: N: g1-f3 is the best move as it maximizes development efficiency, secures the center, and prepares for castling, directly contrasting White's solid play against Black's loose opening setup.\n\nAnswer: N: g1-f3"}
