s to move again. While it provokes weakness, developing the Queen this early is often premature compared to solid central control.\n\nReason: The move **P: d2-d4** is the most principled response. It punishes Black's passive opening by securing space, enabling piece development, and setting up tactical threats along the weakened e8-h5 diagonal if Black tries to contest the center carelessly. It maximizes White's long-term winning chances by establishing a superior position straight from the opening.\n\nAnswer: P: d2-d4"}
